Progress status for this period: insufficient_data. This was computed from strengthIndex by a fixed rule, so treat it as settled and use progressiveOverload.notes to explain it with the specific numbers. The rule: regressing if any muscle group's index dropped 3+ points, otherwise on_track if any rose 3+ points, otherwise stalling; changes are measured against previousIndex, or against 100 (the user's first recorded level) when previousIndex is null.

Analyze this data and submit a structured recommendation using the submit_training_recommendation tool. Base all quantitative judgments strictly on the numbers provided above — do not invent or assume any data not present here. Use the user's age, sex, and BMI, if provided in userContext, to calibrate what counts as reasonable training volume and intensity for their profile. Use strengthIndex as your primary evidence for progressive overload. Use volumeSplit and routineAdherence together to judge balance and consistency. Factor in the user's long-term goal and this period's note (if provided) when shaping your advice and action items. Only make physiological claims that are well established — for example, do not claim that training one muscle group raises hormone levels enough to drive muscle growth elsewhere. If totalSets is 0, note that no training was logged this period rather than speculating why.

Respond in English. Every text field you submit must be plain prose only — no XML tags, no markdown formatting, no stray closing tags of any kind. The headline must stand completely on its own — write it as if it's the only sentence the user will ever read.

{
  "headline": "No strength data was recorded this period, so it's hard to know how your training is really going.",
  "summary": "You logged 12 sets across chest, back, and legs this week, which shows you showed up and put in some work — that's a positive foundation. However, because no strength index data was captured, it's impossible to assess whether your lifts are progressing, stalling, or regressing. The volume split looks reasonably balanced across your three muscle groups, with chest at about 35%, back at 36%, and legs at 29%. For a muscle-building goal at your profile (28-year-old male, 72 kg, BMI 23.5), this is a reasonable starting point, but the total of just 12 sets is on the lower end for hypertrophy. Aim to build on this foundation by increasing total weekly sets and making sure your weights and reps are being tracked so progress can be measured.",
  "progressiveOverload": {
    "notes": "The strength index data is empty for this period, which means progressive overload cannot be assessed from numbers alone. The progress status is therefore marked as insufficient_data — there is simply no index value recorded to compare against a previous level or the baseline of 100. To properly track whether you are progressing toward your muscle-building goal, it is essential to log your working weights and reps consistently each session. Without this, it is impossible to confirm whether you are adding load or volume over time, which is the core driver of muscle growth.",
    "status": "insufficient_data"
  },
  "muscleImbalances": [
    {
      "muscleGroup": "legs",
      "severity": "mild",
      "observation": "Legs account for 29% of total volume this period, slightly below chest (34.8%) and back (36.2%). The gap is not alarming, but for a muscle-building goal the lower body deserves at least equal prioritization over time, as the legs contain some of the largest muscle groups in the body."
    }
  ],
  "deloadRecommended": false,
  "deloadReason": null,
  "actionItems": [
    "Start logging your working weights and rep counts every session — this is the single most important habit change you can make right now, as it is the only way to confirm progressive overload is happening.",
    "Aim to increase your total weekly sets from 12 to at least 15 to 20 sets, distributed across chest, back, and legs, to better support muscle growth at your training age and body size.",
    "Give legs a small volume boost next period — try adding one extra set for legs so it comes closer to matching chest and back proportions.",
    "Pick up where last period's note left off: keep the current progression on your main lifts and continue monitoring recovery, making sure sleep and nutrition are supporting the volume you are putting in."
  ],
  "contextSummary": "Twelve sets logged across chest, back, and legs with a balanced split, but strength index data was absent, making progressive overload unverifiable; priority for next period is consistent weight and rep logging alongside a modest volume increase."
}
